WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.770 --> 00:00:04.350
Over the course of eight seasons of Game of
Thrones, Cersei Lannister managed to get on

00:00:04.350 --> 00:00:07.120
the bad side of just about every other person
in Westeros.

00:00:07.220 --> 00:00:11.320
From complicated schemes to straight up ordering
someone to kill their own dog, here are the

00:00:11.320 --> 00:00:12.520
worst things she ever did.

00:00:12.640 --> 00:00:16.020
And if you're not caught up with this show,
be warned: Spoilers ahead!

00:00:16.300 --> 00:00:21.120
To the bitter end, Cersei proved herself a
master in spectacular feats of totally unnecessary

00:00:21.120 --> 00:00:21.620
sadism.

00:00:21.700 --> 00:00:23.540
"If you have any last words, now is the time."

00:00:24.560 --> 00:00:29.231
Way back in season 1, Cersei established herself
as the cruelest in the Seven Kingdoms: Remember

00:00:29.231 --> 00:00:33.480
when Cersei's son Prince Joffrey Baratheon
gets into a sword fight with Arya Stark, which

00:00:33.480 --> 00:00:36.360
prompts Arya's pet direwolf Nymeria to chomp
on his arm?

00:00:36.400 --> 00:00:38.800
Needless to say, Cersei isn't too thrilled
about this.

00:00:39.080 --> 00:00:43.230
Realizing life could get pretty bad for Nymeria,
Arya sends her away to live in the woods.

00:00:43.230 --> 00:00:47.870
Solid plan…but unfortunately, things don't
work out quite so well for Sansa Stark's pet

00:00:47.870 --> 00:00:48.400
wolf, Lady.

00:00:48.400 --> 00:00:52.540
Since she can't take revenge on Nymeria, Cersei
demands that Lady is killed in her place,

00:00:52.540 --> 00:00:54.600
And Ned insists on doing the deed himself.

00:00:54.740 --> 00:00:59.260
Most of the terrible things Cersei's done
can be seen as protecting her family, consolidating

00:00:59.260 --> 00:01:00.740
her power, or getting payback.

00:01:00.920 --> 00:01:04.519
But ordering the execution of a child’s
pet because of something another’s child’s

00:01:04.520 --> 00:01:05.060
pet did?

00:01:05.100 --> 00:01:06.300
That's just beyond the pale.

00:01:06.800 --> 00:01:11.040
It might be tempting to blame Joffrey's villainy
on Cersei's parenting style, which could charitably

00:01:11.040 --> 00:01:12.640
be described as "indulgent."

00:01:12.800 --> 00:01:16.700
But there's plenty of evidence to suggest
Joffrey's violent tendencies simply run in

00:01:16.700 --> 00:01:17.380
the family.

00:01:17.701 --> 00:01:22.140
Case in point: In the first episode of season
3, a conversation between Cersei and Tyrion

00:01:22.140 --> 00:01:25.920
Lannister dredges up a supremely nasty story
from Cersei's past.

00:01:26.060 --> 00:01:30.160
At the age of nine, she discovered that one
of her servants had stolen a necklace, and

00:01:30.160 --> 00:01:31.900
so she ordered guards to beat the girl.

00:01:32.060 --> 00:01:34.740
They were evidently so thorough, the poor
girl lost an eye.

00:01:34.840 --> 00:01:37.680
When Tyrion reminds her of this fact, Cersei
lights up:

00:01:37.680 --> 00:01:40.520
"If I remember correctly, she never stole
a necklace again."

00:01:40.820 --> 00:01:41.460
In a word?

00:01:41.680 --> 00:01:42.180
Harsh.

00:01:42.400 --> 00:01:45.100
Cersei Lannister isn't a particularly forgiving
soul.

00:01:45.100 --> 00:01:47.480
Just ask the High Sparrow…oh wait, you can't.

00:01:47.600 --> 00:01:51.789
You knew he was in for it once he humiliated
Cersei, parading her through the streets and

00:01:51.789 --> 00:01:54.620
ringing a shame bell all while citizens threw
things at her.

00:01:55.020 --> 00:01:57.420
"Shame, shame…"

00:01:58.340 --> 00:01:59.940
That was never going to end well.

00:02:00.060 --> 00:02:04.279
In one of the show's most deliciously vengeful
moments, Cersei regains control of King's

00:02:04.280 --> 00:02:06.280
Landing in the most brutal way imaginable.

00:02:06.580 --> 00:02:10.800
Using hidden explosives stashed underground,
she blows up the Great Sept of Baelor and

00:02:10.810 --> 00:02:15.140
everyone who happens to be inside it, effectively
putting an end to four ongoing subplots in

00:02:15.140 --> 00:02:16.900
one glorious lime-green fire.

00:02:17.060 --> 00:02:20.840
Of course, blowing up a 200-year-old church
to kill your enemies is generally frowned

00:02:20.840 --> 00:02:22.300
upon in polite society.

00:02:22.530 --> 00:02:27.320
And while she never seemed particularly regretful
about her actions, she did suffer one unforeseen

00:02:27.320 --> 00:02:31.860
consequence: Her son Tommen Baratheon commited
suicide after witnessing the destruction of

00:02:31.860 --> 00:02:36.300
the Sept of Baelor; a catastrophe that majorly
killed his wife, Margaery Tyrell.

00:02:40.860 --> 00:02:44.390
The conflict between the Lannisters and the
Starks is a major focus on Game of Thrones,

00:02:44.390 --> 00:02:48.350
but the rivalry between the Lannisters and
House Martell gave us one of the show's most

00:02:48.350 --> 00:02:49.340
brutal moments.

00:02:49.440 --> 00:02:53.440
It begins with Oberyn Martell, who's killed
in an epic fight with the Mountain while they're

00:02:53.440 --> 00:02:55.780
both acting as proxies in a trial by combat.

00:02:55.960 --> 00:03:00.280
Needless to say, this doesn't sit well with
Oberyn's lover Ellaria, who gets revenge by

00:03:00.290 --> 00:03:02.440
poisoning Cersei's daughter, Myrcella.

00:03:02.440 --> 00:03:06.200
Not surprisingly, Cersei goes to extreme lengths
to avenge her daughter's death.

00:03:06.450 --> 00:03:11.640
Ellaria and her daughter Tyene are soon captured
and taken to King's Landing, where they suffer

00:03:11.640 --> 00:03:13.880
a terrible fate, courtesy of one Cersei Lannister.

00:03:14.120 --> 00:03:18.680
Instead of merely executing them, Cersei has
Ellaria and Tyene chained up in the dungeon,

00:03:18.680 --> 00:03:19.440
facing one another.

00:03:19.460 --> 00:03:21.800
Then, she poisons Tyene...with a kiss.

00:03:21.860 --> 00:03:26.360
It's the same kind of poison Ellaria used
on Myrcella, and it takes a verrrry long time

00:03:26.360 --> 00:03:27.440
to kill its victims.

00:03:27.550 --> 00:03:31.980
Cersei leaves Ellaria alive…and she promises
that her soldiers will keep her alive, just

00:03:32.000 --> 00:03:35.290
to ensure she sees every grisly second of
her daughter's death.

00:03:35.580 --> 00:03:38.060
"You will live to watch your daughter rot.

00:03:39.060 --> 00:03:42.240
To watch that beautiful face collapse."

00:03:42.360 --> 00:03:43.360
Vintage Cersei.

00:03:43.360 --> 00:03:43.920
'Nuff said.

00:03:44.480 --> 00:03:47.640
Things started going downhill for the Stark
family when Ned agreed to become Hand of the

00:03:47.640 --> 00:03:49.060
King for Robert Baratheon.

00:03:49.060 --> 00:03:52.660
But the brakes were well and truly off when
Robert died, leaving Ned and the kids with

00:03:52.670 --> 00:03:54.270
no allies in King's Landing.

00:03:54.270 --> 00:03:58.010
Although it probably goes without saying,
Cersei orchestrated Robert's death.

00:03:58.010 --> 00:04:00.020
That's not to say she didn't have her reasons:

00:04:00.160 --> 00:04:03.140
"I killed my husband because it felt good
to be rid of him."

00:04:03.360 --> 00:04:03.980
Fair enough.

00:04:03.980 --> 00:04:07.900
Well, there's another level of terribleness
to this unfortunate business: Cersei's scheme

00:04:07.900 --> 00:04:09.600
is just so needlessly complicated.

00:04:10.030 --> 00:04:14.760
In lieu of far easier methods like poisoning
or beheading, Cersei had to get fancy, opting

00:04:14.760 --> 00:04:19.300
for an over-the-top assassination plot that
requires multiple steps and multiple players.

00:04:19.400 --> 00:04:20.540
Here's how it all goes down.

00:04:20.600 --> 00:04:23.759
After seducing her cousin Lancel that's step
one!

00:04:23.760 --> 00:04:27.340
Cersei gets him to deliver far too much wine
to Robert while he's out hunting.

00:04:27.420 --> 00:04:29.500
"We were hunting…a boar…"

00:04:30.420 --> 00:04:34.020
What he's trying to say here is: Robert got
too drunk to hunt, which sounds like the beginning

00:04:34.020 --> 00:04:37.800
of some Jeff Foxworthy joke, and wound up
getting gored by a wild boar.

00:04:38.000 --> 00:04:39.780
All part of Cersei's masterful plan!

00:04:39.920 --> 00:04:42.460
As Robert dies, the absurdity isn't lost on
him:

00:04:42.760 --> 00:04:47.940
"King Robert Baratheon…murdered by a pig."

00:04:48.240 --> 00:04:52.340
That means Cersei relied on three other living
creatures to pull off her scheme…including

00:04:52.340 --> 00:04:53.940
Robert and a very large pig.

00:04:54.120 --> 00:04:58.780
And yet…her terrible plan somehow totally
worked, which only makes it twice as terrible.

00:04:58.880 --> 00:05:00.120
That's so Cersei.

